From ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com  Thu Aug 20 17:27:49 2015
From: ua5e83218c834e013 at groups.google.com (Michael Betancourt)
Date: Thu, 20 Aug 2015 17:27:49 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] What's a good way to incorporate rounding in measurement in STAN?
Message-ID: <KIH26xC_3UM.mUVocr4cBgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <KIH26xC_3UM.8_by3QziBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <KIH26xC_3UM.IokWMv3MIwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <KIH26xC_3UM.8_by3QziBQAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/KIH26xC_3UM/m/mUVocr4cBgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Basically write your model as usual and then to incorporate
rounding integrate the likelihood across the founding bins
(say, x - 0.5, x + 0.5).  Your model will then become a
multinomial distribution with probabilities given by the
likelihood integrated across those bins.

On Aug 19, 2015, at 4:32 PM, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:

> That won't work as written.
> > On Aug 19, 2015, at 6:41 PM, Daniel Lakeland <dlak...@street-artists.org> wrote:
> >
> >
> >
> > On Tuesday, August 4, 2015 at 10:12:40 PM UTC-7, dynotec wrote:
> > If I have repeated measurements of a binomial proportion, then a natural way to model it would be with something like:
> >
> > stan_data <- list(
> > num_obs=3,
> > y = c(0,0,0.01),
> > sample_size=c(300,300,300)
> > )
> > stan_code <- '
> >  data {
> > int num_obs;
> >    real y[num_obs]; // Observed residuals
> > int sample_size[num_obs];
> >  }
> >  parameters {
> > real<lower=0,upper=1> p;
> >  }
> >  model {
> > real y_sigma[num_obs];
> >
> > for(j in 1:num_obs)
> > y_sigma[j] <- sqrt(p*(1-p)/sample_size[j]);
> >    y ~normal(p ,y_sigma) ;
> >  }
> > '
> >
> > But how would I modify this code if I knew that the data I was getting was being rounded to the nearest integer percentage?
> >
> >
> > How about
> >
> > parameters {
> > real yact[num_obs];
> > }
> >
> > model{
> > yact ~ uniform(y-.005,y+.005);
> > ...
> > yact ~ normal(p,y_sigma)
> > }
> >
> > ???
> >
>
> That won't work as written, because yact is unconstrained, but
> has support only on y +/- 0.005.
>
> If you want to treat the true value as missing data and assume
> the measurement error is uniform, you could do this so that
> the support matches the constraints (the uniform on y_err is
> implicit):
>
> data {
>  ...
>  vector[N] y_obs;
> }
> parameters {
>  ...
>  vector<lower=-0.005, upper=0.005>[N] y_err;
> }
> transformed parameters {
>  ...
>  vector[N] y;
>  ...
>  y <- y_obs + y_err;
> }
> model {
>  ...
>  y ~ normal(mu_y, sigma_y);
> }
>
> where y_meas is the measurement of y.
>
> This is another case where the parser's going to spit out
> a Jacobian warning that you don't need to worry about.
>
> But there may be rounding error plus measurement error, in which case,
> it might make sense to just roll the rounding error in with the rest of
> the error and not try to build an explicit measurement error model.
>
> - Bob
>

