I figured as much. So basically it is an intractable problem due to the data volume, even with the optimisations suggested (the final dataset would be 1200 x 80million, give or take, with about 1,5million parameters). Could you recommend any alternative strategies? Or is there a way to only compute the expression for theta_hat as needed, instead of pre-allocating all of the memory needed for all expressions?

I’ve also been considering if its actually possible to just fit the model to one event, and then use the posterior as a prior for the next model, and so forth, so I would only need to do 1x 280k iterations per model. Though I am not sure if that is actually sensible.

Thanks.

David

> On 21 Aug 2015, at 17:07, Bob Carpenter <ca...@alias-i.com> wrote:
>

> It's the loop in your model.
>
> You have a D x N loop, so that's 255 * 280K = 71 million iterations, each of which
> then creates an expression for theta_hat and for the bernoulli_logit that's going
> to suck up lots of memory.  It's basically 40 bytes or so per binary subexpression in
> the model and you get around 5 per iteration, for another 200 bytes * 71 million
> = 14 GB.  The 19GB is almost certainly because we allocate in blocks, rather than
> a byte at a time in order to ensure it only takes logarithmic time.
>
> You could save a bit of space by turning (beta_age * age) into a dot-product and
> by defining a vector of theta_hat[n] and then vectorizing bernoulli_logit, but it's
> still going to take a lot of memory.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On Aug 21, 2015, at 9:09 AM, David Westergaard <da...@harsk.dk> wrote:
