From: u8aa9b8cc06fdc99c at groups.google.com (Bob Carpenter)
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2015 22:01:38 +0000
Subject: Re: [stan-users] What's a good way to incorporate rounding in measurement in STAN?
Message-ID: <KIH26xC_3UM.BJ_MQkR6BgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
In-Reply-To: <KIH26xC_3UM.spkBIKF5BgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
References: <KIH26xC_3UM.IokWMv3MIwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com> <KIH26xC_3UM.spkBIKF5BgAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/KIH26xC_3UM/m/BJ_MQkR6BgAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Stan assumes support on the constrained parameter space.  That
is, everywhere the parameter meets its constraints, it should
have density > 0.

If that is violated, then multiple things might happen.  In the
best case, you'll init near the solution and the sampler won't
move near the boundaries.  If you had a big vector of y ~ uniform(-1,1)
and used our random inits, you're very unlikely to be able to move.

Michael will have to weigh in on what happens with bias in cases
where things run without crashing or getting stuck.

- Bob

