> Nope, if it’s rounded data, my model is right and Bob’s is wrong.  And my model can be expressed using latent data to also give inference for the underlying weights  and the actual roundoff errors.

There is only one underlying weight, so your model and bob's model both give inference for that (mu). But Bob's model also gives inference for what the individual measurement errors were, and yours only gives inference for what the standard deviation of the pre-rounded measurement errors is.

His model is perfectly fine, it's just a model for jointly: an underlying weight, and a vector of roundoff errors.

