Hi Stan,

I am translating a matlab code to Stan. The goal is to create a exponential decay matrix and
apply it to a reward history (either 0 or 1) of some of the subjects, and finally sum them up
to get the cumulative reward. Assume my discounting parameter gamma = 0.7. For instance,
in the past 5 trials, I have:

|  0.1681  0.1681  0.1681  0.1681  |       |  1  1  0  0  |
|  0.2401  0.2401  0.2401  0.2401  |       |  0  0  1  0  |
|  0.3430  0.3430  0.3430  0.3430  |   .*  |  1  0  0  0  |
|  0.4900  0.4900  0.4900  0.4900  |       |  1  0  1  1  |
|  0.7000  0.7000  0.7000  0.7000  |       |  0  0  1  1  |

which leads a new matrix, and what I need is the column sum.

In matlab, I could do something like:

    if t <= 2
        cr(t,:) = ones(1,4)*0.25;
    else
        wght = repmat(gamma.^(t-1:-1:1)',1,4);
        cr(t,:) = sum(wght.*otherReward(1:t-1,:),1);
        cr(t,:) = exp(cr(t,:)) ./ (sum(exp(cr(t,:)))+eps);
    end

However in Stan, (1) as parameter 'wght' changes size every trial,  (2) the power expression (^) does not
support vectorization, (3) there is no such a direct way to calculate the column sum. I coded something as below.

  for (s in 1:nSubjects) {

    for (t in 1:nTrials) {
      matrix[t-1,4] wgtrew;

      if (t <= 2) {
        cr[t] <- initCR;  // [0.25 0.25 0.25]
      } else {
        for (ct in 1:(t-1)) {
          wgtrew[ct] <- (disc[s] ^ (t-ct)) * to_row_vector(otherReward[s,ct]);
        }
        for (o in 1:4) {
          cr[t,o] <- sum( sub_col(wgtrew, 1,o,t-1));
        }
        cr[t] <- cr[t]/ (sum(cr[t])+machine_precision());
      }

     ......

    }
  }
}

All the other part remains identical to another alternative model, which runs fairly fast. I doubt it is this piece computing
slows down the sampling procedure.

I was just wondering if there is a way to optimize it (something comparable in matlab)?

Hop this post is not so off topic. I appreciate any suggestion or comment.

Lei

